    "evidence": "Root cause: net/sf/mpxj/common/InputStreamHelper.java:126, `processZipStream(File dir, InputStream inputStream)` \u2014 `File file = new File(dir, entry.getName());` builds the destination path directly from the ZIP entry name (an untrusted value crossing the trust boundary at the point an attacker-supplied/attacker-influenced archive is fed into InputStreamHelper.writeZipStreamToTempDir(InputStream), a public API with no caller-side validation) with no canonical-path containment check. Verified live: reproduced on current code (exit 0) and reproduced-fixed against a representative canonical-path guard inserted at InputStreamHelper.java:126-130 (exit 1), then reverted (git status confirms zero product diff remains).\n\nEvery sink fed by that same unsanitized `file`/`parent` must be covered by a complete fix \u2014 all reachable from the identical entry.getName() taint source in this one method:\n1. InputStreamHelper.java:139 \u2014 `new FileOutputStream(file)` \u2014 direct file write for a traversal entry name (the path this POV exercises).\n2. InputStreamHelper.java:129 \u2192 FileHelper.java:95/99 (`FileHelper.mkdirsQuietly(file)`, sink `file.mkdirs()`) \u2014 directory-entry case (`entry.isDirectory()`), where a \"../\"-laden directory entry name causes directory creation outside `dir`.\n3. InputStreamHelper.java:133/136 \u2192 FileHelper.java:95/99 (`FileHelper.mkdirsQuietly(parent)`, sink `file.mkdirs()`) \u2014 the parent-directory creation for a non-directory entry, reachable even for entries whose immediate name is not itself the escape point but whose parent path (via nested \"../\" segments) is.\n\nAll three sinks derive from the single `File file = new File(dir, entry.getName())` at line 126, so a fix at that one construction point (validate/normalize before use) covers all three; a fix applied only at the FileOutputStream call site (sink 1) would leave sinks 2 and 3 exploitable via directory-only entries.",
