    "pov_path": ".security-pipeline/pov/ZipSlipPovTest.java",
    "regression_hint": "Invariant: for every ZipEntry extracted in InputStreamHelper.processZipStream, the resolved destination (both the entry's own File and, for non-directory entries, its parent File) must have a canonical path that is equal to, or a strict descendant of, the canonical path of the temp extraction `dir` returned by writeZipStreamToTempDir \u2014 i.e. `file.getCanonicalPath().startsWith(dir.getCanonicalPath() + File.separator)` (or the file being extracted equals dir itself, though that's not a realistic case). Any entry that fails this check must be rejected (throw, or skip the entry) before any of mkdirsQuietly(file) [line 129], mkdirsQuietly(parent) [line 136], or `new FileOutputStream(file)` [line 139] is invoked \u2014 checking only one of the three sinks is insufficient since directory entries and file entries take different branches from the same tainted `file`/`parent` values.\n\nKnown bypass variants the fix must also block, not just simple leading \"../\":\n- Nested/mid-path traversal: \"sub/../../evil.txt\" (parent-directory computation at line 133/136 still escapes even if the literal entry name doesn't start with \"../\").\n- Directory-entry traversal: a ZipEntry with isDirectory()==true and a \"../\"-laden name, exercised only via the mkdirsQuietly(file) sink at line 129 (never reaches FileOutputStream, so a fix guarding only the file-write branch misses this).\n- Absolute path entry names (e.g. \"/etc/cron.d/evil\" or Windows \"C:\\\\evil\"), where `new File(dir, entry.getName())` \u2014 because Java's File 2-arg constructor treats an absolute second argument as replacing `dir` entirely on some scenarios/platforms \u2014 can resolve completely outside `dir` regardless of \"..\" segments.\n- Backslash-encoded traversal on platforms/JVMs that treat \"\\\\\" as a separator, or URL/percent-encoded segments if any decoding occurs upstream of this method.\n- Symlink-based escapes: even a name that stays lexically under `dir` could still escape if an intermediate path component is a symlink created by an earlier entry in the same archive \u2014 canonicalization (getCanonicalPath, not just getAbsolutePath/normalize) is required to catch this, and ordering-dependent symlink-then-traverse sequences within one archive should be considered.",
